Write A=(3,5), B=(5,11) and C=(4,8); then AB=\sqrt{40}=2\sqrt{10} while the required sum is 4\sqrt{5}=\sqrt{80}>AB so the second locus is an ellipse centred at C
Here semi-major a=2\sqrt{5} and focal half-distance c=\sqrt{10}, giving semi-minor b=\sqrt{a^2-c^2}=\sqrt{10}
Thus the ellipse is centred at the circle centre with minor radius equal to the circle radius \sqrt{10}, meeting it exactly at the two minor-axis ends → (B)

